                  }
                }
              },
              "answerArea": {
                "calculator": false,
                "type": "multiple"
              },
              "hints": [
                {
                  "content": "Consider how the bacterial population changes after each hour. If it starts at 100, what is the population after 1 hour? What about after 2 hours? Look for a consistent pattern in how the initial population is multiplied over time.",
                  "images": {},
                  "replace": false,
                  "widgets": {}
                },
                {
                  "content": "When a quantity consistently doubles (or multiplies by the same factor) over equal time intervals, it indicates exponential growth. The general formula for exponential growth is $P(t) = P_0 \\cdot r^t$, where $P_0$ is the initial quantity, $r$ is the growth factor (the factor by which it multiplies each interval), and $t$ is the number of time intervals.",
                  "images": {},
                  "replace": false,
                  "widgets": {}
                },
                {
                  "content": "In this problem, the initial population ($P_0$) is 100 bacteria. Since the population doubles every hour, the growth factor ($r$) is 2. Substitute these values into the exponential growth formula $P(t) = P_0 \\cdot r^t$ to find the expression for $N$ after $t$ hours.",
